From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 10:39:08 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] tcp_input: leave a connect to port 0 unanswered

A guest SYN whose connect() fails at once is answered with ICMP
net-unreachable, so the guest sees ENETUNREACH within milliseconds. A
BSD host stack (Darwin) refuses port 0 that way: connect() to port 0
fails with EADDRNOTAVAIL before anything is sent. Linux, the guest's own
stack and the model for this network, sends the SYN, so a destination
that does not answer leaves the guest connect pending until its timeout.
Libcore's socket test, which connects to an unroutable address on port
0 and then reads the socket's timeout, saw the socket already failed.

Drop the SYN without a reply when the failure is EADDRNOTAVAIL and the
destination port is 0: tcp_close and m_free discard it, and the guest
retransmits and times out as it does on a real network. Every other
synchronous failure keeps its ICMP error. A host that does exist would
have answered port 0 with a reset; the host stack gives no way to tell.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/tcp_input.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/tcp_input.c b/src/tcp_input.c
index 6617d3845adf5dfff0af0a7ec77950c3a6d94222..38a841a1ad84af86220d1f53412789a4766d558e 100644
--- a/src/tcp_input.c
+++ b/src/tcp_input.c
@@ -628,6 +628,16 @@ findso:
                 /* ACK the SYN, send RST to refuse the connection */
                 tcp_respond(tp, ti, m, ti->ti_seq + 1, (tcp_seq)0,
                             TH_RST | TH_ACK, af);
+            } else if (errno == EADDRNOTAVAIL &&
+                       (af == AF_INET ? so->so_fport : so->so_fport6) == 0) {
+                /*
+                 * A BSD host stack refuses a connection to port 0 before it
+                 * sends anything. A stack that sent the SYN would hear
+                 * nothing from an unreachable host, and the guest would
+                 * retransmit until it gave up. Answering with an ICMP error
+                 * instead failed the connect at once. Leave the SYN
+                 * unanswered; tcp_close and m_free below discard it.
+                 */
             } else {
                 switch (af) {
                 case AF_INET:
